A||N||3||Monkeyman||Trouble changing fastback trunk lock cyl||||8||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||11-29-2001||06:00 PM||||The lock cyl broke the other day so I bought a new one.  Just had it keyed to match my door keys.  I tried to remove the 4 screws that seem to hold the lock assy in place but all I could loosen were the back 2 (towards the back of the car not the back of the assy).  I've sprayed some penetrating oil on them, but I'm to the point of stripping out the screwdriver hole (the X that the screwdriver fits in).  I know I could drill the screws out, but will that destroy the threads or can I use some slightly larger, self tapping screws?  I've about had it with my car (nothing major...always little things I can't seem to fix on my own).  I'd like to fix a few of these little things before I attempt to sell it.||||reg||8
Z||000001||Monkeyman||11-29-2001||08:05 PM||||bump||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||11-30-2001||08:45 AM||||Try using a dremmel tool and slot the screw heads for a flat tip screwdriver. Sometimes the combination of heat, vibration and the added torque from a flat tip can make a large difference.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bill Osmer||11-30-2001||09:20 AM||||MM -<P>There are small "easy-outs" that you can get from your local hardware store.<P>I changed out my lock, and experienced similar rust and difficauylty. Mostly, I used penetrating oil, and patience (allowing the Liquid Wrench to penetrate and do its job).<P>Drilling off the screw heads works, too, but then you'll have to remove the "stump" that remains behind.<P>Good luck<P>Bill||||reg||4
